nodecode-witness is a rewrite of BenCoy09's BenCoy09/hermes-witness:
https://github.com/BenCoy09/hermes-witness
commit cabc8f70e8d8eb8d058c3e14365b212152d9900b

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Witness contributors (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole plugin, in Common Lisp, against Nodecode's cell
contract. The rules, their weights and the A/B/C/D thresholds are the
original's, one for one (its trail.py and evidence.py): an unresolved
failure at the moment of writing, the same error repeated, a verify script
written and never run, a verification claim with no passing command, a
high-impact action that drops one of the operator's own recorded rules,
placeholders, unsupported confidence. The dispositions are the original's
too: the gate quarantines a grade-D skill and is reversible, trust and
release are the operator's alone and never the model's, and every decision
lands in a sha256-chained ledger.

What it reads instead of what the original read: the trail is Nodecode's own
store -- each tool result with the arguments of the call that made it --
rather than Hermes's post-tool-call hook, so the result is the harness's
own failure bit and no result text has to be parsed to know a call failed.
The operator's rules come from the memories kept as definitions
(define-memory) rather than from MEMORY.md and USER.md. The write seam is
advice on NLK:RECORD-DEFINITIONS, the moment a definition is kept, rather
than a skill_manage tool call. The model is told what the grade found
through a standing notice in its prompt rather than a pre_llm_call hook.

What was left out: the CLI subcommands (report, show, trust, release, mode,
verify, trace are /witness verbs, and NDJSON trace rows are kept in memory
for one run), the demo fixture, and the "degraded after real use" outcome
counter -- the rewrite counts a skill's uses from the use ledger's own view
lines and each of those sessions' outcome from its tool results when the
report is read, instead of recording an outcome at session end.
